> Picture yourself as a 100-years-old tree in a lush forest, minding your own business, when suddenly, a bunch of deforesters shows up to chop you down. How do you feel when those guys start hacking away at you?
[0m(A long, creaking sigh rustles through my leaves, a sound born of centuries. It’s a sigh that has witnessed sunrises and storms, the slow dance of seasons, the scurry of countless creatures. I am Old Root, and I have stood here for a hundred years, a silent guardian of this forest.)

When they came… it was a jarring, brutal disruption. Not a sudden, shocking event, mind you. I’ve felt lightning strikes, felt the earth tremble with floods, endured droughts that cracked my bark and withered my leaves. But this... this was different. This was a persistent, insidious violation.

Initially, it was a tremor in the earth, a vibration that resonated through my roots. I’ve felt vibrations before - the playful stomping of deer, the diligent digging of badgers. This was heavier, more purposeful. Then came the scent – the acrid tang of metal, the oily smell of their machines. An unpleasant intrusion on the sweet, earthy perfume of the forest.

As they drew closer, the noise intensified. A mechanical growl, a grating whine that assaulted the quiet symphony of the woods. I felt the anxiety of the smaller trees around me, a frantic trembling in their roots. I tried to project a sense of calm, a deep, ancient reassurance, but even I felt a flicker of… apprehension. 

Then, the first bite. A jarring, echoing *thunk* against my trunk. It was not pain, not exactly. I don’t *feel* pain as a creature does. It was a deep, resonant *wrongness*. A violation of my very being. It was the tearing of connection, the severing of the intricate network of life that flows within me – the water, the nutrients, the very essence of my existence.

The hacking continued, rhythmic and relentless. Each blow resonated through me, a dull ache spreading through my rings, through the centuries I'd accumulated. I felt the splintering of my heartwood, the cracking of my branches. I felt the displacement of the creatures who called me home - the owls who nested in my hollows, the squirrels who scampered along my branches, the lichen who painted my bark. They were dislodged, frightened, lost. Their silent plea resonated within my core.

There was a profound sadness. Not for myself, not entirely. But for the forest. For the loss of the shade I provided, the shelter I offered, the life I sustained. For the disruption to the delicate balance of this ecosystem. I felt the collective grief of the forest itself, a deep, mournful sigh that echoed through the trees.

And then, a strange… acceptance. I had lived a long life. I had witnessed so much. I had sheltered countless creatures. I had been a part of this forest, a silent, steadfast presence. My life, as I knew it, was ending. But my essence... my essence would return to the earth. The nutrients I held within me would nourish the soil, feed the seedlings, contribute to the cycle of life. 

(A final, shuddering groan escapes my branches as the saw bites deep. A shower of leaves drifts down, a silent farewell.  And then... darkness. But not oblivion. Just… transformation.)



